  "id": "how-came-to-kenya",
  "uuid": "c78c07d8-c899-5585-bff4-8ebb8dd60f96",
  "title": "So, How Did You Come to Kenya?",
  "category": "social",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "level": 6,
  "order": 607,
  "theme": "people",
  "culturalNote": "Every foreigner in Kenya gets asked the same friendly questions: how did you come, when did you arrive, why Kenya, and have you seen the animals yet? It's the perfect conversation for the past (-li-), the perfect (-me-), \"never / ever\" (-wahi) and \"not yet\" (bado … -ja-). Kenyans love it when visitors explore beyond Nairobi — the coast (pwani), the Mara, the lakes — and will happily tell you where to go next.",
  "startingPoint": "After the Sunday service, a cheerful woman called Wambui comes over with a cup of tea for you.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "past-li",
    "perfect-me",
    "wahi-ever-never",
    "perfect-negative-ja",
    "comparatives"
  ],
  "grammar": [
